22. OFFLINE-FIRST OPERATION

A.R.G.U.S. must continue to provide useful operational capability when internet connectivity is lost.

Each device should maintain a durable local replica/cache.

Offline operations create signed events and store them in a durable outbox.

Conceptual flow:

User action
→ local validation
→ local state update
→ signed event
→ durable outbox
→ offline pending status

When connectivity returns:

outbox
→ synchronization
→ validation
→ remote/shared event publication
→ reconciliation
→ confirmation

The app must visibly distinguish:

LOCAL
QUEUED
SYNCING
SYNCHRONIZED
CONFLICT
FAILED

---

23. OFFLINE CONFLICT RESOLUTION

A.R.G.U.S. must never silently hide conflicts.

Example:

Only 1 Medium SDB jacket remains.

Device A offline:
issues final jacket to Cadet A.

Device B offline:
also issues final jacket to Cadet B.

Upon synchronization:

A.R.G.U.S. identifies a conflict.

It must create a reconciliation workflow showing:

- competing events
- time recorded
- actors
- affected inventory
- resulting impossible state

An authorized user resolves the conflict.

Resolution generates another signed event.

The system must never silently discard one transaction.

